<Site
  title="About · Ouijit"
  description="Ouijit is built by one person, Prentice Bjerkeseth. Free, open source under AGPL, no account and no telemetry, and the plan is to keep it that way.">
  <main class="compare-content about-content">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      Ouijit is a free, open source tool for running coding agents in parallel, built and maintained by one
      person. Most tools in this space are raising venture money, getting acquired, or shutting down. Ouijit's
      aim is to still be standing, still free and still private, and to show that an individual can build
      something special here.
    </p>
    <p>
      It began as play. Once AI got good enough to build with, I started building something with it for fun,
      and within a week it was the only way I worked. That was possible because a tool that runs the agents in
      real terminals is exactly as capable as the agents themselves on day one, and gets better as they do.
      Capabilities still land at the terminal first, so Ouijit wraps around the agent's own CLI rather than
      replacing it. A chat interface is coming; the terminal stays underneath.
    </p>
    <p>
      Being one person shapes the rest. There is no account, because a tool like this shouldn't need a login.
      There is no telemetry, because you shouldn't trade privacy for good tools; the only number I have is how
      many times each build has been downloaded. It is free, and if anything ever costs money it will be a
      feature with a running cost of its own, like cloud environments or syncing, never the app. And it is
      open source under AGPL-3.0, so it stays open and isn't easily taken by a company and sold back.
    </p>
    <p class="about-sign"><a href="https://github.com/pbjer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prentice Bjerkeseth</a></p>
  </main>
</Site>
